{
  "concept": "Whether a solid conducts electricity depends on the size of the band gap between its filled valence band and empty conduction band — the smaller the gap, the easier it is to promote electrons and the better it conducts.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "title 'Insulator' above left panel",
    "title 'Semiconductor' above middle panel",
    "title 'Conductor' above right panel",
    "blue-violet rectangle labeled 'Valence' in each panel (lower band)",
    "green rectangle labeled 'Conduction' in each panel (upper band)",
    "white gap region between bands with vertical dashed left/right edges in each panel",
    "double-headed vertical arrow spanning the gap, labeled 'Band gap' (Insulator and Semiconductor panels)",
    "'Band gap' label placed outside the Conductor panel to the right (gap too narrow for an internal label)",
    "electron promotion arrow (appears on interaction) crossing or failing to cross the gap",
    "energy axis cue: energy increases upward (implicit in vertical stacking)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "bandGapSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Band gap size",
      "range": [0, 10, 0.5],
      "default": 5,
      "teaches": "Band gap is a continuous quantity, and the labels conductor / semiconductor / insulator are just regions along that continuum — shrinking the gap moves a material from insulator toward conductor"
    },
    {
      "id": "panelClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Promoting an electron from the valence to the conduction band requires energy at least equal to the gap — a small gap is crossed easily, a huge gap blocks promotion, which is why insulators don't conduct"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Compare the three panels",
      "narration": "All three diagrams are built identically — valence band below, conduction band above. The only thing that changes is the height of the white band gap: largest for the insulator, half as big for the semiconductor, nearly zero for the conductor.",
      "state": { "bandGapSlider": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Try to promote an electron",
      "narration": "Click each panel to attempt an electron promotion. In the conductor the electron crosses almost effortlessly; in the insulator the arrow falls far short — the gap is the energy barrier an electron must overcome.",
      "state": { "bandGapSlider": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep the gap",
      "narration": "Drag the slider to shrink and grow the gap and watch the material's label switch between conductor, semiconductor, and insulator — the categories are points on one continuous scale of band-gap energy.",
      "state": { "bandGapSlider": 2 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Electrical conduction in a solid is governed by a single number — the band gap energy — because an electron can only carry current after being promoted across that gap from the filled valence band into the conduction band.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three side-by-side panels sharing identical block widths and colors: blue-violet 'Valence' block at bottom, green 'Conduction' block above, white gap between them edged with vertical dashed lines. Insulator (left) has the tallest gap with a double-headed arrow and 'Band gap' label inside; Semiconductor (middle) has roughly half that gap, same arrow construction, and its whole stack sits slightly lower; Conductor (right) has only a thin white sliver, with its 'Band gap' label placed outside to the right. Panel titles in bold above each. For the interactive version, one panel can be driven by the slider while the three canonical examples remain as reference; on click, animate an electron (small dot with upward arrow) from the valence block — it lands in the conduction band when the gap is small and stalls/falls back when the gap is large, with a proportional energy marker beside it."
}

